# Reusable formulas for the scenarios: ubc user functions that any model can
# call. Defining them prints nothing. Every built-in scenario loads them
# first; load them before your own model, in the same command, so the
# functions flow into it:
#
#   ubc --scenario <area>/<name>
#   ubc --scenario formulas my-model.ubc
#
# Every argument can be a plain number or an uncertain one ("a to b"). Keep
# the time units consistent: a monthly rate with a number of months, a weekly
# cost of delay with a duration in weeks.

# People

# Loaded cost of a person: what the company pays, not just the salary.
# Taxes, benefits, equipment and office usually add 25-40% on top of the
# salary, so overhead is a factor around 1.25 to 1.4.
loaded_cost(salary, overhead) = salary * overhead

# Real capacity of a team in person-days: the time left for planned work
# once meetings, support, interruptions and holidays are taken out. The focus
# factor (the share of the time that goes to planned work) is commonly
# 0.5 to 0.8 in Scrum teams.
real_capacity(people, days, focus) = people * days * focus

# Flow

# Little's law (John Little, 1961): in a stable system, the average lead time
# is the work in progress divided by the throughput. With 20 items in
# progress and 4 finished a week, an item takes 5 weeks.
lead_time(wip, throughput) = wip / throughput

# Cost of delay (Don Reinertsen, The Principles of Product Development Flow):
# the value lost for every period a feature is not in users' hands, times the
# periods it is delayed.
delay_cost(cost_of_delay, periods) = cost_of_delay * periods

# CD3, cost of delay divided by duration (Reinertsen; Joshua Arnold): do
# first the work with the highest value lost per unit of time spent on it.
cd3(cost_of_delay, duration) = cost_of_delay / duration

# Product

# Customer lifetime value: the margin a customer brings each period, over
# the expected lifetime. With a constant churn rate per period, a customer
# stays 1 / churn periods on average (a 2% monthly churn means 50 months).
ltv(revenue_per_period, gross_margin, churn) = revenue_per_period * gross_margin / churn

# CAC payback: the periods it takes for a customer's margin to pay back what
# it cost to acquire them. Under 12 months is the usual SaaS benchmark.
cac_payback(cac, revenue_per_period, gross_margin) = cac / (revenue_per_period * gross_margin)

# Risk

# Annual loss expectancy (quantitative risk analysis, as in NIST SP 800-30):
# how often an incident is expected in a year times what one incident costs.
# A probability of 0.1 a year of a 200k incident is a 20k expected loss.
annual_loss(probability, impact) = probability * impact

# Availability of two services a request needs both of (in series): both
# must be up, so the availabilities multiply. Chain calls for more services:
# availability_series(a, availability_series(b, c)).
availability_series(a, b) = a * b

# Availability of two redundant services where either one is enough (in
# parallel): it is down only when both are down at once, assuming they fail
# independently.
availability_parallel(a, b) = 1 - (1 - a) * (1 - b)

# Money over time

# Compound growth: a value that grows by rate every period, for a number of
# periods (a 10% yearly growth for 2 years multiplies by 1.21).
compound(value, rate, periods) = value * (1 + rate) ^ periods

# Present value: what an amount received some periods from now is worth
# today, discounted at rate per period (the cost of capital, often 8-12% a
# year).
present_value(amount, rate, periods) = amount / (1 + rate) ^ periods

# Net present value of an investment paid today that returns the same cash
# every period, for a number of periods, discounted at rate per period (the
# present value of an annuity minus the investment). Positive means it pays
# off. The rate must not be 0.
npv(investment, cash_per_period, rate, periods) = cash_per_period * (1 - (1 + rate) ^ -periods) / rate - investment
